  ]
 },
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Legend says this sword could cut silk in mid-air... and it was born in India.",
   "emph": [
    "silk",
    "india"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The world called it Damascus steel.",
   "emph": [
    "damascus"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Its real name was woots, probably from ukku, a South Indian word for steel.",
   "caption": "Its real name was WOOTZ, probably from UKKU, a South Indian word for steel.",
   "emph": [
    "wootz",
    "ukku"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Smiths in South India sealed iron and plant matter in small clay pots, and fired them for hours...",
   "emph": [
    "clay",
    "pots"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "until a glowing cake of steel was born.",
   "emph": [
    "steel"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Traders shipped these cakes to Persia and Syria,",
   "emph": [
    "persia",
    "syria"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "where smiths forged them into blades with a flowing, water-like pattern.",
   "emph": [
    "water-like"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "But by the eighteen hundreds, the furnaces went cold, and the exact method was lost.",
   "caption": "But by the 1800s, the furnaces went cold, and the exact method was lost.",
   "emph": [
    "lost"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Even Michael Faraday studied it, in eighteen nineteen, trying to copy it.",
   "caption": "Even Michael Faraday studied it, in 1819, trying to copy it.",
   "emph": [
    "faraday",
    "1819"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Then in two thousand six, scientists reported something inside a seventeenth-century Damascus sabre... carbon nanotubes.",
   "caption": "Then in 2006, scientists reported something inside a 17th-century Damascus sabre... carbon nanotubes.",
   "emph": [
    "2006",
    "carbon",
    "nanotubes"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The world called it Damascus. It began in India.",
   "emph": [
    "india"
   ]
  }
 ],
